## Security & Credential Storage

**Responsibility:** Protect credentials through approved native storage, controlled access, explicit error behavior, and a managed key lifecycle.

| Concern | Android | iOS | React Native |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Credential storage | Approved storage adapter with Android Keystore integration | Scoped Keychain items with reviewed accessibility/access controls | Approved native-backed auth adapter |
| Cryptographic keys | Keystore; hardware backing depends on device/key configuration | Keychain and supported Secure Enclave key operations | Delegates to native platform; JSI is not key protection |
| Biometric access | Policy-backed authentication with supported authenticators | LocalAuthentication / Keychain access controls as appropriate | Native adapter exposes the chosen policy |
| Cached personal data | Reviewed database/file protection and key lifecycle | Data protection, storage policy, and scoped cleanup | Approved cache encryption and key management |
| Network security | Approved TLS client and centrally maintained policy | Approved TLS client and platform trust evaluation | Native networking adapter follows the same policy |

Keystore manages keys; it is not an arbitrary token database. Keychain can store small credential values, while Secure Enclave supports particular key operations—it does not mean every generic-password item is an enclave-held key. A native interface or JSI call does not itself supply hardware-backed encryption. See [Apple's Keychain storage guidance](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/cryptokit/storing-cryptokit-keys-in-the-keychain).

### Credential contract

Use equivalent behavior across platforms: return “absent” only when an item is absent; propagate storage, access-control, corruption, and authentication failures distinctly. Writes must not erase the previous credential before a replacement is safely stored. Deletion is restricted to the application's account namespace.

**Android — `core/security/SecureCredentialStore.kt`**

```kotlin
package com.enterprise.mobile.core.security

data class CredentialScope(val accountRef: String)
enum class CredentialName { REFRESH_TOKEN }

interface SecureCredentialStore {
    suspend fun read(scope: CredentialScope, name: CredentialName): ByteArray?
    suspend fun write(scope: CredentialScope, name: CredentialName, value: ByteArray)
    suspend fun removeAccountCredentials(scope: CredentialScope)
}
```

Implementations must be main-safe and cancellation-aware. Treat the returned byte array as sensitive mutable data: avoid unnecessary copies, do not log it, and do not claim that clearing one array guarantees all runtime copies are erased.

**iOS — `Core/Security/SecureCredentialStore.swift`**

```swift
import Foundation

struct CredentialScope: Sendable {
    let accountRef: String
}
enum CredentialName: String, Sendable {
    case refreshToken
}
protocol SecureCredentialStore: Sendable {
    func read(scope: CredentialScope, name: CredentialName) async throws -> Data?
    func write(scope: CredentialScope, name: CredentialName, value: Data) async throws
    func removeAccountCredentials(scope: CredentialScope) async throws
}
```

A native Keychain adapter scopes queries by service, account, class, and access group where applicable. Treat `errSecItemNotFound` as absence; other statuses require handling. Prefer a scoped update/add strategy over unconditional delete-then-add. Select accessibility deliberately: background access, device lock, migration, backup, and biometric enrollment requirements differ.

**React Native — `src/core/security/secureCredentialStore.ts`**

```typescript
export type CredentialName = 'refreshToken';
export interface CredentialScope {
  readonly accountRef: string;
}
export interface SecureCredentialStore {
  read(scope: CredentialScope, name: CredentialName): Promise<string | null>;
  write(scope: CredentialScope, name: CredentialName, value: string): Promise<void>;
  removeAccountCredentials(scope: CredentialScope): Promise<void>;
}
```

Bind this contract to the approved native-backed SDK. Do not implement it with a hardcoded MMKV encryption key or store credentials in Redux, AsyncStorage, or a general profile cache. Encrypted MMKV may be used for approved cache data with a managed key; it is not automatically the credential-storage implementation.

<Aside type="caution" title="Choose supported implementations and explicit policy">
Android's `security-crypto` APIs, including `EncryptedSharedPreferences` and `MasterKey`, are deprecated. Do not copy them as a new universal foundation. Follow the approved supported adapter and migration plan; plain SharedPreferences does not become secure credential storage because an older wrapper is deprecated. See the [Android Security library release notes](https://developer.android.com/jetpack/androidx/releases/security).
</Aside>

### TLS and biometric policy

- Apply TLS trust evaluation centrally. Never ship sample pins, disable hostname verification, or install a trust-all manager.
- If enterprise policy requires pinning, maintain reviewed production/backup pins, rotation ownership, expiry behavior, and a recovery plan. Pinning is an additional operational commitment, not a substitute for TLS trust evaluation. See [Apple's identity-pinning guidance](https://developer.apple.com/news/?id=g9ejcf8y).
- Distinguish a biometric prompt from a cryptographic operation protected by biometric authentication. Define lockout, cancellation, unavailable hardware, enrollment changes, and any permitted device-credential fallback.
- Keep server credentials and private backend secrets out of mobile binaries. Store only the client credentials the approved flow actually requires.

**Review:** Missing item, device locked, user cancellation, key invalidation, write failure, account isolation, backup/restore, and deletion failure all have explicit behavior.


### Android · Keystore-backed credential adapter

`core/security/AndroidCredentialStore.kt`

This API 23+ reference uses an account-specific Keystore AES key and authenticated encryption. Ciphertext files live in the app's no-backup directory. It is a single-process, process-scoped adapter; share one instance so its mutex covers all access. The approved SDK may implement these responsibilities differently.

```kotlin
package com.enterprise.mobile.core.security

import android.content.Context
import android.security.keystore.KeyGenParameterSpec
import android.security.keystore.KeyProperties
import android.util.AtomicFile
import java.io.File
import java.io.IOException
import java.nio.ByteBuffer
import java.security.KeyStore
import java.security.MessageDigest
import javax.crypto.Cipher
import javax.crypto.KeyGenerator
import javax.crypto.SecretKey
import javax.crypto.spec.GCMParameterSpec
import kotlinx.coroutines.CoroutineDispatcher
import kotlinx.coroutines.sync.Mutex
import kotlinx.coroutines.sync.withLock
import kotlinx.coroutines.withContext

class AndroidCredentialStore(
    context: Context,
    private val ioDispatcher: CoroutineDispatcher,
) : SecureCredentialStore {
    private val root = File(context.applicationContext.noBackupFilesDir, "credentials")
    private val mutex = Mutex()

    private fun accountHash(scope: CredentialScope): String {
        require(scope.accountRef.isNotBlank())
        return MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA-256")
            .digest(scope.accountRef.toByteArray(Charsets.UTF_8))
            .joinToString("") { "%02x".format(it.toInt() and 0xff) }
    }
    private fun alias(hash: String) = "enterprise.credentials.$hash"
    private fun keyStore() = KeyStore.getInstance("AndroidKeyStore").apply { load(null) }
    private fun existingKey(hash: String): SecretKey? =
        keyStore().getKey(alias(hash), null) as? SecretKey

    private fun writeKey(hash: String): SecretKey = existingKey(hash) ?: run {
        val generator = KeyGenerator.getInstance(KeyProperties.KEY_ALGORITHM_AES, "AndroidKeyStore")
        generator.init(KeyGenParameterSpec.Builder(
            alias(hash), KeyProperties.PURPOSE_ENCRYPT or KeyProperties.PURPOSE_DECRYPT,
        ).setBlockModes(KeyProperties.BLOCK_MODE_GCM)
            .setEncryptionPaddings(KeyProperties.ENCRYPTION_PADDING_NONE)
            .setRandomizedEncryptionRequired(true).setKeySize(256).build())
        generator.generateKey()
    }

    override suspend fun write(scope: CredentialScope, name: CredentialName, value: ByteArray) =
        withContext(ioDispatcher) {
            mutex.withLock {
                require(value.size <= 16_384) { "Credential exceeds supported size." }
                val hash = accountHash(scope)
                val directory = File(root, hash)
                if (!directory.isDirectory && !directory.mkdirs()) throw IOException("Credential directory unavailable.")
                val cipher = Cipher.getInstance("AES/GCM/NoPadding")
                cipher.init(Cipher.ENCRYPT_MODE, writeKey(hash))
                check(cipher.iv.size == 12) { "Unsupported GCM nonce size." }
                cipher.updateAAD("$hash:${name.name}".toByteArray(Charsets.UTF_8))
                val encrypted = cipher.doFinal(value)
                val record = ByteBuffer.allocate(2 + cipher.iv.size + encrypted.size)
                    .put(1.toByte()).put(cipher.iv.size.toByte()).put(cipher.iv).put(encrypted).array()
                val file = AtomicFile(File(directory, name.name))
                val stream = file.startWrite()
                try {
                    stream.write(record)
                    file.finishWrite(stream)
                } catch (error: Exception) {
                    file.failWrite(stream)
                    throw error
                }
            }
        }

    override suspend fun read(scope: CredentialScope, name: CredentialName): ByteArray? =
        withContext(ioDispatcher) {
            mutex.withLock {
                val hash = accountHash(scope)
                val file = AtomicFile(File(File(root, hash), name.name))
                // openRead also recovers an interrupted AtomicFile write when applicable.
                val bytes = try {
                    file.openRead().use { input ->
                        val buffer = ByteArray(16_513)
                        var count = 0
                        while (count < buffer.size) {
                            val next = input.read(buffer, count, buffer.size - count)
                            if (next < 0) break
                            count += next
                        }
                        if (count == buffer.size) throw IOException("Credential record exceeds limit.")
                        buffer.copyOf(count)
                    }
                } catch (missing: java.io.FileNotFoundException) {
                    if (file.baseFile.exists() || File(file.baseFile.path + ".bak").exists()) throw missing
                    return@withLock null
                }
                val record = ByteBuffer.wrap(bytes)
                if (record.remaining() < 2 || record.get().toInt() != 1) throw IOException("Unsupported credential record.")
                val ivSize = record.get().toInt() and 0xff
                if (ivSize != 12 || record.remaining() < ivSize + 16) throw IOException("Invalid credential record.")
                val iv = ByteArray(ivSize).also { record.get(it) }
                val encrypted = ByteArray(record.remaining()).also { record.get(it) }
                val key = existingKey(hash) ?: throw IOException("Credential key unavailable.")
                val cipher = Cipher.getInstance("AES/GCM/NoPadding")
                cipher.init(Cipher.DECRYPT_MODE, key, GCMParameterSpec(128, iv))
                cipher.updateAAD("$hash:${name.name}".toByteArray(Charsets.UTF_8))
                cipher.doFinal(encrypted)
            }
        }

    override suspend fun removeAccountCredentials(scope: CredentialScope) = withContext(ioDispatcher) {
        mutex.withLock {
            val hash = accountHash(scope)
            val directory = File(root, hash)
            var removed = false
            try {
                removed = !directory.exists() || directory.deleteRecursively()
            } finally {
                keyStore().deleteEntry(alias(hash))
            }
            if (!removed) throw IOException("Credential files could not be removed.")
        }
    }
}
```

**Hint:** Never create a replacement key while reading an existing ciphertext record. Missing/invalidated keys and authentication failures must reach recovery. The reference does not request StrongBox or biometric-bound keys and therefore makes no universal hardware/biometric guarantee. Add those policies in the approved adapter, including unavailable-hardware behavior. Do not copy this as a second vault when an enterprise SDK already owns credentials. `AtomicFile` requires callers to serialize access; it is not a multi-process lock. See the [AtomicFile contract](https://developer.android.com/reference/android/util/AtomicFile).

### iOS · scoped Keychain implementation

`Core/Security/KeychainCredentialStore.swift`

```swift
import Foundation
import Security

enum CredentialStoreError: Error {
    case status(OSStatus)
    case malformedValue
    case invalidScope
}

actor KeychainCredentialStore: SecureCredentialStore {
    private let servicePrefix: String

    init(servicePrefix: String) {
        self.servicePrefix = servicePrefix
    }

    private func service(_ scope: CredentialScope) throws -> String {
        guard !scope.accountRef.isEmpty else { throw CredentialStoreError.invalidScope }
        return "\(servicePrefix).\(scope.accountRef.utf8.count):\(scope.accountRef)"
    }
    private func query(_ scope: CredentialScope, _ name: CredentialName) throws -> [String: Any] {
        [kSecClass as String: kSecClassGenericPassword,
         kSecAttrService as String: try service(scope),
         kSecAttrAccount as String: name.rawValue,
         kSecAttrSynchronizable as String: false]
    }

    func read(scope: CredentialScope, name: CredentialName) async throws -> Data? {
        try Task.checkCancellation()
        var request = try query(scope, name)
        request[kSecReturnData as String] = true
        request[kSecMatchLimit as String] = kSecMatchLimitOne
        var result: CFTypeRef?
        let status = SecItemCopyMatching(request as CFDictionary, &result)
        if status == errSecItemNotFound { return nil }
        guard status == errSecSuccess else { throw CredentialStoreError.status(status) }
        guard let data = result as? Data else { throw CredentialStoreError.malformedValue }
        return data
    }

    func write(scope: CredentialScope, name: CredentialName, value: Data) async throws {
        try Task.checkCancellation()
        let search = try query(scope, name)
        let attributes: [String: Any] = [
            kSecValueData as String: value,
            kSecAttrAccessible as String: kSecAttrAccessibleWhenUnlockedThisDeviceOnly,
        ]
        var status = SecItemUpdate(search as CFDictionary, attributes as CFDictionary)
        if status == errSecItemNotFound {
            var item = search
            attributes.forEach { item[$0.key] = $0.value }
            status = SecItemAdd(item as CFDictionary, nil)
            // Handles another cooperating writer adding the item between update and add.
            if status == errSecDuplicateItem {
                status = SecItemUpdate(search as CFDictionary, attributes as CFDictionary)
            }
        }
        guard status == errSecSuccess else { throw CredentialStoreError.status(status) }
    }

    func removeAccountCredentials(scope: CredentialScope) async throws {
        try Task.checkCancellation()
        let request: [String: Any] = [
            kSecClass as String: kSecClassGenericPassword,
            kSecAttrService as String: try service(scope),
            kSecAttrSynchronizable as String: false,
        ]
        let status = SecItemDelete(request as CFDictionary)
        guard status == errSecSuccess || status == errSecItemNotFound else {
            throw CredentialStoreError.status(status)
        }
    }
}
```

**Hint:** This policy allows access only while unlocked and prevents migration of these items to another device. Background token access may need a different reviewed policy. Add an explicit access-group restriction when using shared Keychain groups. The actor serializes this adapter's access; Keychain APIs remain synchronous system calls. Do not invoke them in a tight UI loop or assume an actor alone guarantees a particular background thread. See [Keychain update/delete behavior](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/security/updating-and-deleting-keychain-items).

### React Native · native Keychain adapter

`src/core/security/nativeCredentialStore.ts`

This implementation uses the documented `react-native-keychain` 9.x-style API contract, including its `false` results; adapt the calls to the version approved in the application. See the [package API documentation](https://oblador.github.io/react-native-keychain/docs/9.2.x/api/functions/getGenericPassword/). If the enterprise auth SDK already owns credential access, bind the same contract to that SDK instead.

```typescript
import * as Keychain from 'react-native-keychain';
import type { CredentialName, CredentialScope, SecureCredentialStore } from './secureCredentialStore';

const credentialNames: readonly CredentialName[] = ['refreshToken'];

export class NativeCredentialStore implements SecureCredentialStore {
  constructor(private readonly servicePrefix: string) {}

  private service(scope: CredentialScope, name: CredentialName): string {
    if (!scope.accountRef.trim()) throw new Error('Credential scope is required.');
    return `${this.servicePrefix}:${encodeURIComponent(scope.accountRef)}:${name}`;
  }

  async read(scope: CredentialScope, name: CredentialName): Promise<string | null> {
    const item = await Keychain.getGenericPassword({ service: this.service(scope, name) });
    if (item === false) return null;
    if (item.username !== name) throw new Error('Unexpected credential record.');
    return item.password;
  }

  async write(scope: CredentialScope, name: CredentialName, value: string): Promise<void> {
    const result = await Keychain.setGenericPassword(name, value, {
      service: this.service(scope, name),
      accessible: Keychain.ACCESSIBLE.WHEN_UNLOCKED_THIS_DEVICE_ONLY,
    });
    if (result === false) throw new Error('Credential write did not complete.');
  }

  async removeAccountCredentials(scope: CredentialScope): Promise<void> {
    let failed = false;
    for (const name of credentialNames) {
      const service = this.service(scope, name);
      try {
        if (await Keychain.getGenericPassword({ service }) !== false) {
          if (!await Keychain.resetGenericPassword({ service })) failed = true;
        }
      } catch {
        failed = true; // Attempt all registered names; report overall failure to caller.
      }
    }
    if (failed) throw new Error('Credential cleanup is incomplete.');
  }
}
```

**Hint:** Keep one registered credential inventory and update cleanup when adding a credential name. Serialize writes with session cleanup through the application coordinator. Do not place a token returned by this adapter in Redux or diagnostic output. Confirm native setup and the package's supported version/platform options; an iOS accessibility setting is not proof of equivalent Android hardware properties.


